00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.750
<v Orlando>Welcome, everybody, to grammar lesson number two.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.800
<v Orlando>My name is Orlando Kelm here at the
University of Texas with my partners.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.250
<v José Luis>José Luis Montiel.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.850
<v Valdo>Valdo Oliveira.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:23.910
<v Michelle>Michelle Lima.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:29.191
<v Orlando>Michelle Lima, as always. Okay, welcome to another
lesson. Today we're going to talk about contractions.

00:00:29.191 --> 00:00:31.002
<v Orlando>You know, all the time people ask me,

00:00:31.002 --> 00:00:35.750
<v Orlando>is it true that Brazilians understand Spanish
better than Spanish speakers understand Portuguese?

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.550
<v Valdo>It's true.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.550
<v Michelle>It's true.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.550
<v Valdo>That is the truth. Yes, indeed.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.250
<v Orlando>And I think today's lesson is one of the reasons why.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:50.500
<v Orlando>There are a million contractions between prepositions and little
tiny words that just makes things fly by for Spanish speakers.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:52.270
<v Valdo>Right.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:52.560
<v Michelle>Exactly.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:01:00.550
<v Orlando>So for example, the word "in the", <lang pt-BR>em</lang> <lang pt-BR>o</lang>, is
pronounced <lang pt-BR>no</lang>, <lang pt-BR>no</lang>, <lang pt-BR>no livro</lang>, "in the book".

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.250
<v Orlando>And so I think for a Spanish speaker, they're used to
hearing <lang es>en el libro</lang>, and they get all of a sudden <lang pt-BR>no livro</lang>.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.200
<v Orlando>I think your brain goes, "What
happened? It just went by me real fast."

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.200
<v Orlando>I think, I really do.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.364
<v Orlando>I think that one of the reasons why it's more difficult
to understand, and there are other reasons too,

00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:20.750
<v Orlando>is that there are many, many contractions
in Portuguese that do not exist in Spanish.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.300
<v Orlando>Spanish has <lang es>del</lang> for <lang es>de el</lang>

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.050
<v Orlando>and <lang es>al</lang> for <lang es>a el</lang>.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.250
<v Orlando>But in general, we don't see as many contractions in Spanish.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.240
<v Orlando>Today's dialogue we're going to talk about
getting change from a machine in a supermarket.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.870
<v Orlando>And as we listen to it, we're going to
hear words like <lang pt-BR>do</lang>, <lang pt-BR>da</lang>, <lang pt-BR>no</lang>, <lang pt-BR>na</lang>, <lang pt-BR>numa</lang>, <lang pt-BR>num</lang>,

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>nesses</lang>, <lang pt-BR>nessas</lang>, <lang pt-BR>naqueles</lang>, <lang pt-BR>naquelas</lang>.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:51.126
<v Orlando>And so we'll talk about them after we do the dialogue, but
just to get your ear ready for it, as we hear the dialogue,

00:01:51.126 --> 00:01:52.250
<v Orlando>focus on contractions.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.550
<v Orlando>And they're always with those little tiny words.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.500
<v Orlando>And with that, Michelle, you're going to talk about
getting change out of a machine at a supermarket.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.670
<v Michelle>Right.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.250
<v Orlando>We'll let you have at it.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.480
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Ficar na fila é duro, né?</lang>

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.800
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Que tal se a gente passar pelo meio e chegar naquele outro caixa?</lang>

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.000
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Do lado de lá? Tá bom.</lang>

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:20.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Viu, às vezes me confundo com o troco nesses supermercados.</lang>
<lang pt-BR>Por que eles nunca colocam as moedas nas nossas mãos?</lang>

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.900
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Porque as moedas caem das maquininhas que ficam ao lado do caixa.</lang>

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.360
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Ah, tá... é que nos supermercados brasileiros a</lang>
<lang pt-BR>gente recebe todo o troco dos próprios caixas.</lang>

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:39.531
<v Orlando>Nice dialogue. And as we heard, just tons of these little tiny
contractions: "in the", "on the", "of the", "from the", "by the",

00:02:39.531 --> 00:02:40.750
<v Orlando>those sort of things.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.965
<v Orlando>So as we get ready to listen to it again, these contractions
are made of prepositions with articles, like "the",

00:02:47.965 --> 00:02:52.397
<v Orlando>and like demonstrative pronouns, "this",
"that", "those", those kind of words.

00:02:52.397 --> 00:02:58.250
<v Orlando>So just to kind of get your ear going for it, if I
wanted to say "in the book", that'd be <lang pt-BR>em</lang>, <lang pt-BR>o</lang>, <lang pt-BR>livro</lang>.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:03.050
<v Orlando>But the combination becomes…

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>No livro.</lang>

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.950
<v Orlando><lang pt-BR>No livro</lang>, where in Spanish that would be…

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.500
<v José Luis><lang es>En el libro.</lang>

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:14.610
<v Orlando>If I wanted to say "in the letter", that'd
be <lang pt-BR>em</lang>, <lang pt-BR>a</lang>, <lang pt-BR>carta</lang>. And you would then say…

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.750
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Na carta.</lang>

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.550
<v Orlando>Okay, so that gives a combination. In Spanish, it'd be…

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.500
<v José Luis><lang es>En la carta.</lang>

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:29.050
<v Orlando>So you have to get used to the idea of <lang pt-BR>em</lang>,
<lang pt-BR>a</lang>, <lang pt-BR>na</lang>, <lang pt-BR>em</lang>, <lang pt-BR>o</lang>, <lang pt-BR>no</lang>. And in the plural as well:

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.550
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Em</lang>, <lang pt-BR>os</lang>, <lang pt-BR>nos</lang>.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.550
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Em</lang>, <lang pt-BR>as</lang>, <lang pt-BR>nas</lang>.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.110
<v Orlando>And so you can see that's really different that way.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.230
<v Orlando>Now with the preposition of, it's the same thing.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:43.030
<v Orlando>If I want to say "of the man", that'd be <lang pt-BR>de</lang>, <lang pt-BR>o</lang>, <lang pt-BR>homem</lang>.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.300
<v Orlando>And put them together, you'd put?

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:45.300
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Do homem.</lang>

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.750
<v Orlando>Where in Spanish, that'd be…

00:03:48.350 --> 00:03:49.590
<v José Luis><lang es>Del hombre.</lang>

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:50.300
<v Orlando><lang es>Del hombre</lang>, right?

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:57.050
<v Orlando>So there's that combination in Spanish. But if I say
"of the woman", <lang pt-BR>de</lang>, <lang pt-BR>a</lang>, <lang pt-BR>mulher</lang>, that would come out…

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.300
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Da mulher.</lang>

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:00.600
<v Orlando>But in Spanish it would be?

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.500
<v José Luis><lang es>De la mujer.</lang>

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:03.750
<v Orlando>And so that's the whole idea here.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.250
<v Orlando>It also happens with words like this and these.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:13.800
<v Orlando>So if I wanted to say "in that book",
it'd be <lang pt-BR>em esse livro</lang>. That becomes?

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.050
<v Michelle><lang pt-BR>Nesse livro.</lang>

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.050
<v Orlando>Where in Spanish it would be?

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.300
<v José Luis><lang es>En ese libro.</lang>

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:19.000
<v Orlando><lang es>En ese libro</lang>, right?

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.250
<v Orlando>And so that's, again, the same idea.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:25.050
<v Orlando>And it also works with the word <lang pt-BR>aquele</lang>,
<lang pt-BR>naquele</lang>, <lang pt-BR>daquele</lang>, and all those sort of things.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:32.505
<v Orlando>And so as you listen to our lesson, this will really
